SECTION 44 of Administrators General Act, 1963
44. Auditors to examine accounts and report to Government.-
The auditors shall examine the accounts and forward to the State Government a statement thereof in the prescribed form, together with a report thereon and a certificate signed by them showing-
(a) whether the accounts have been audited in the prescribed manner;
(b) whether, so far as can be ascertained by such audit, the accounts contain a full and true account of everything which ought to be inserted therein;
(c) whether the books which by any rules made under this Act are directed to be kept by theAdministrator-General, have been duly and regularly kept; and
(d) whether the assets and securities have been duly kept and invested and deposited in the manner prescribed by this Act, or by any rules made thereunder;
or (as the case may be) that such accounts are deficient, or that the Administrator-General has failed to comply with this Act or the rules made thereunder, in such respects as may be specified in such certificate.

simplified_text:
Simplified text: Auditing Accounts – Section 44 of Administrators General Act, 1963
44. State Government Reporting Requirements:

The auditors are responsible for reviewing the accounts. They must submit a statement to the State Government in the required format, along with a report and a signed certificate stating:
(a) if the accounts were audited according to the regulations;
(b) whether the accounts provide a complete and accurate record of all necessary information;
(c) if the Administrator-General maintained the required books as per the Act's rules;
(d) if assets and securities were handled and invested as directed by the Act or its rules, or if there are any deficiencies or failures to comply with the Act or rules, as specified in the certificate.

Simplified text: Offenses During Military Service:
Under the Air Force Act, 1950, any person subject to this law who commits certain offenses may face harsher penalties when on active duty compared to other times. These offenses include:
Forcing a safeguard, or using force against a sentry;
Breaking into a building to steal;
Falling asleep or being drunk while on sentry duty;
Leaving assigned duty without permission;
Causing a false alarm or spreading alarming rumors;
Revealing or manipulating military passwords without authorization;
Interfering with air signals without permission.
Penalties: If convicted by a military court for these offenses while on active service, the offender could be imprisoned for up to fourteen years or face a lesser punishment as outlined in the law. If the offense occurs while not on active service, the maximum imprisonment term can be up to seven years or a lesser penalty.

